  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Saturday)\nIssued at 1248 AM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nAn upper level ridge across the region should result in dry weather \nMonday, with a warming 850 mb thermal ridge resulting in warmer \nhighs for Monday in the upper 70s to mid 80s, despite the \nnortheasterly surface winds continuing. Weakening of the ridge will \noccur Tuesday and Wednesday, while a surface high pushing into the \narea will bring light easterly surface winds. Continued warming of \nthe 850 mb thermal ridge will cause the warming trend to continue \n(highs in the low to upper 80s Tuesday, mid 80s to low 90s \nWednesday). \n\nA cutoff upper low looks to develop somewhere across West Texas by \nThursday and linger through Friday and perhaps Saturday as well, \nbefore being pushed east by a longwave upper trough moving into the \nWest. The cutoff low lingering over the area will bring large scale \nascent and also draw in some increasing moisture into the region. \nThis would likely bring some shower and thunderstorm activity to \nWest Central Texas. Although some cooling will likely occur Thursday \ninto Friday/Saturday, there is a lot of uncertainty in how much \ncooling will occur, as it will be highly dependent on the location \nof the exact location of the cutoff low and subsequent magnitude of \nmoisture/clouds/rainfall.",
